    <section id="penalties">
      <h2>Penalties: the cost of doing nothing</h2>
      <p>Cabinet Decision No. 106 of 2025 sets the e-invoicing penalties. They apply once your phase starts, and they are on top of normal VAT penalties.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap"><table class="w700"><thead><tr><th>Violation</th><th>Penalty</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Not implementing e-invoicing or not appointing an ASP on time</td><td>AED 5,000 for each month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Not issuing or sending an e-invoice</td><td>AED 100 per invoice, up to AED 5,000 a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Not issuing or sending an e-credit note</td><td>AED 100 per credit note, up to AED 5,000 a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Not telling your ASP about changes to your business data in time</td><td>AED 1,000 for each day</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p>Waiting is not free. Six months without an ASP after the deadline can cost <b>AED {fmt(penalty6)}</b> in fines alone, far more than setting up e-invoicing.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="scope">
      <h2>Who must use e-invoicing</h2>
      <ul class="know">
        <li><b>In scope:</b> invoices between businesses (B2B) and to government (B2G) in the UAE.</li>
        <li><b>Excluded for now:</b> sales to consumers (B2C).</li>
        <li><b>Other exclusions:</b> some transactions are excluded, such as certain government sovereign activities, certain international airline services and exempt financial services.</li>
        <li><b>Tax groups:</b> transactions between members of the same tax group are excluded for two years from 1 January 2027, under the October 2026 amendments.</li>
        <li><b>Not sure?</b> If you are VAT-registered and sell to other businesses in the UAE, assume it applies to you and check your case.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="basics">
      <h2>E-invoicing basics in plain words</h2>
      <ul class="know">
        <li><b>E-invoice:</b> an invoice sent as structured data (an XML file), not a PDF. The UAE format is called <b>PINT AE</b>.</li>
        <li><b>ASP:</b> the accredited provider that checks your invoice, converts it to PINT AE and sends it. You appoint your ASP on <b>EmaraTax</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Peppol:</b> the international network the invoices travel on. When you register, your business gets a <b>Peppol ID</b>, its address on the network.</li>
        <li><b>How an invoice travels:</b> you create it, your ASP checks and sends it to your customer's ASP, which delivers it to your customer, and the data is reported to the Federal Tax Authority (FTA).</li>
        <li><b>Both sides need an ASP:</b> the same ASP handles the invoices you send and the ones you receive.</li>
        <li><b>Mistakes:</b> a sent e-invoice cannot be edited. You correct it with an <b>e-credit note</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Records:</b> keep your e-invoice records for the period in the Tax Procedures Law (generally 5 years) and give them to the FTA on request. The October 2026 amendments removed the rule that the data must be stored inside the UAE.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

